    <string name="quote_text_1">We cannot say that the life given to us is short, only that we waste most of it.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_1">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_2">People are extremely stingy in protecting their personal property; yet when it comes to wasting time, they are extremely wasteful of the one thing in which it is right to be stingy.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_2">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_3">If you want to find peace, do less. Or rather, do only what is necessary. Ask yourself at every moment: \'Is this really necessary?\'</string>
    <string name="quote_author_3">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_4">What a person has in himself, what accompanies him when he is alone, is far more important than what others think of him.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_4">Arthur Schopenhauer</string>
    <string name="quote_text_5">Running from place to place and constantly distracting your attention is a sign of a diseased mind.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_5">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_6">My experience is what I agree to attend to.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_6">William James</string>
    <string name="quote_text_7">Your soul is dyed by the color of your thoughts.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_7">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_8">Of all ridiculous things, the most ridiculous seems to me to be busy—to be a man who is always busy with work and hustle.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_8">Søren Kierkegaard</string>
    <string name="quote_text_9">Nothing we have is ours; nature has only entrusted us with this slippery and volatile thing we call time.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_9">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_10">All sins are attempts to fill the voids within us.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_10">Simone Weil</string>
    <string name="quote_text_11">He who has a \'why\' to live for can bear almost any \'how\'.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_11">Friedrich Nietzsche</string>
    <string name="quote_text_12">The value of your attention to any action should be in direct proportion to the importance of that action.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_12">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_13">The unhappy man is always absent from himself; he can never exist within himself, he is always missing in the moment.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_13">Søren Kierkegaard</string>
    <string name="quote_text_14">The life of the busy man is filled with everything but living.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_14">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_15">A man can be himself only so long as he is alone; if he does not love solitude, he will not love freedom; for it is only when he is alone that he is really free.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_15">Arthur Schopenhauer</string>
    <string name="quote_text_16">You live as if you were destined to live forever, no thought of your mortality ever enters your head; you pay no attention to how much time you waste.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_16">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_17">Things outside our minds do not affect us directly. It is how our mind judges those things that shapes our feelings.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_17">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_18">We are what we repeatedly do. Excellence, then, is not an act, but a habit.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_18">Will Durant</string>
    <string name="quote_text_19">Every habit and faculty is confirmed and strengthened by the corresponding actions; walking by walking, running by running.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_19">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_20">If you do not wish to do something, refrain from doing it and learn to make something else a habit instead.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_20">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_21">We are spinning our own fates, good or evil, never to be undone. Every smallest stroke of virtue or of vice leaves its never-so-little scar on our nerve cells.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_21">William James</string>
    <string name="quote_text_22">The faculty of voluntarily bringing back a wandering attention over and over again is the very root of judgment, character, and will.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_22">William James</string>
    <string name="quote_text_23">Do every day a very little but difficult thing you do not want to do, simply for the sake of training your will; so that when a moment of great difficulty approaches, you are not caught unprepared.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_23">William James</string>
    <string name="quote_text_24">No man is free who is not master of himself.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_24">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_25">We cannot cure our mistakes by sheer willpower and gritting our teeth, but only by giving them our full attention.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_25">Simone Weil</string>
    <string name="quote_text_26">Virtues are formed in man only by actually practicing them.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_26">Aristotle</string>
    <string name="quote_text_27">When you are captivated by the allure of a promised pleasure, guard yourself from being swept away by it.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_27">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_28">It is surprising to see how quickly an unfed desire dies.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_28">William James</string>
    <string name="quote_text_29">It is not of small importance whether we form habits of one kind or another from our youth; it is very important, or rather, all-important.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_29">Aristotle</string>
    <string name="quote_text_30">Freedom is secured not by the fulfilling of desires, but by the removal of desire.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_30">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_31">Addiction is the greatest producer of illusions; only those who can rid themselves of addictions can reach reality.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_31">Simone Weil</string>
    <string name="quote_text_32">Concentrate every minute like a Roman—on doing the job in front of you with perfect and simple dignity, affection, and justice; free yourself from all other distractions.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_32">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_33">People who labor all their lives but have no purpose to direct all their thoughts and impulses waste their time, even if they work hard.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_33">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_34">By abstaining from pleasures we become temperate, and once we have become temperate we are most able to abstain from them.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_34">Aristotle</string>
    <string name="quote_text_35">The body must be treated more rigorously, so that it does not disobey the mind.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_35">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_36">The hours are flowing by, and they are all charged to our account.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_36">Martial</string>
    <string name="quote_text_37">How much longer will you delay demanding the best for yourself and refusing to break the rules of right reason?</string>
    <string name="quote_author_37">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_38">Now is the time to accept everything that is best for you as a law that you will never break.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_38">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_39">While we waste our time hesitating and procrastinating, life goes on.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_39">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_40">Do not say \'I will live tomorrow\'; tomorrow\'s life is too late; live today.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_40">Martial</string>
    <string name="quote_text_41">Remember that there is a limit to the time allotted to you to free yourself, and if you do not use it, it will fly away, never to return.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_41">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_42">No one can build you the bridge upon which you must cross the river of life, except you yourself alone.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_42">Friedrich Nietzsche</string>
    <string name="quote_text_43">We are responsible to ourselves only for our own existence; therefore, we want to be the real pilots of this existence, we cannot let it resemble a thoughtless accident.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_43">Friedrich Nietzsche</string>
    <string name="quote_text_44">Think of yourself as dead. You have lived your life. Now take what\'s left and live it properly.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_44">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_45">The greatest obstacle to living is expectancy, which hangs upon tomorrow and wastes today. You are arranging what lies in Fortune\'s control, and abandoning what lies in yours.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_45">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_46">Whenever you say, \'I will let it go today, I will pay attention tomorrow,\' know that you have said this: \'Today I will be shameless, impudent, and base; today I will be angry and a slave to others.\' See what evil you present to yourself!</string>
    <string name="quote_author_46">Epictetus</string>
    <string name="quote_text_47">Look back and remember: How many people plundered your life without you even realizing... When you see how little is left of your time, you will realize you are dying untimely.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_47">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_48">Losing oneself in the multiplicity of choices is the nature of aesthetic choice; whereas unless a person chooses himself absolutely, he only makes momentary and temporary decisions.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_48">Søren Kierkegaard</string>
    <string name="quote_text_49">He who lives longest and he who will die soonest lose the same thing: Only the present moment. For you possess only it, and you cannot lose what you do not have.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_49">Marcus Aurelius</string>
    <string name="quote_text_50">It is terrifying to be left alone with oneself as both judge and executioner of the law one has set.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_50">Friedrich Nietzsche</string>
    <string name="quote_text_51">Imagination continually fills up all the cracks so that reality cannot enter.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_51">Simone Weil</string>
    <string name="quote_text_52">The life of those who forget the past, neglect the present, and fear the future is extremely short and full of anxiety.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_52">Seneca</string>
    <string name="quote_text_53">It is disgraceful to deal with difficult but trivial things; and labor spent on childish matters is foolishness.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_53">Martial</string>
    <string name="quote_text_54">The contest has begun; you are now at the Olympic Games, you have no option to delay any longer, and a single defeat can ruin all your progress.</string>
    <string name="quote_author_54">Epictetus</string>
